WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.415
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:07.390
SPEAKER: In these
courses, we deconstructed

00:00:07.390 --> 00:00:11.140
the API product, introduced
the API product mindset,

00:00:11.140 --> 00:00:13.690
reviewed the customer experience
with customer journeys

00:00:13.690 --> 00:00:16.210
and personas, and
now, we would like

00:00:16.210 --> 00:00:18.940
to offer a few tips on how
to formulate your API product

00:00:18.940 --> 00:00:22.900
strategy, starting with the
design of your API products.

00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:26.410
Recall the keys to an
API product mindset.

00:00:26.410 --> 00:00:29.870
Approach the design with
the consumer in mind,

00:00:29.870 --> 00:00:33.080
enable fast time to market
with reusable assets,

00:00:33.080 --> 00:00:34.760
and don't boil the ocean.

00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:37.370
But rather, iterate to
continuously improve

00:00:37.370 --> 00:00:39.480
the product.

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:41.970
API products are your
digital product line.

00:00:41.970 --> 00:00:45.540
And like any product line, it
can target different consumers

00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:48.000
by adding small
tweaks to the product.

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:51.360
You should treat each API as
you would any other product you

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:54.070
produce in your organization.

00:00:54.070 --> 00:00:56.130
One of the simplest
examples of a product

00:00:56.130 --> 00:00:58.650
is made by the Coca-Cola
beverage company.

00:00:58.650 --> 00:01:01.530
The company originally came
out with the Coca-Cola soda

00:01:01.530 --> 00:01:04.349
and over the past 90
years have branched out

00:01:04.349 --> 00:01:07.380
into fitness beverages,
teas, juices,

00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:09.620
and different types of water.

00:01:09.620 --> 00:01:12.120
Each product has its
own target audience.

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:16.040
For example, Adwala, Simply
Orange, and Minute Maid

00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:19.880
are beverages targeted at
families with young children.

00:01:19.880 --> 00:01:22.340
Fair Life and Core Power
are targeted at the health

00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:24.590
conscious and fitness fanatics.

00:01:24.590 --> 00:01:27.650
And the Coca-Cola company
also offers a variety

00:01:27.650 --> 00:01:31.010
of water beverages, including
Smart Water, Vitamin Water,

00:01:31.010 --> 00:01:33.110
and ZICO coconut water.

00:01:33.110 --> 00:01:36.063
Similarly, your API
product strategy

00:01:36.063 --> 00:01:38.480
will have different product
offerings for different target

00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:39.410
audiences.

00:01:39.410 --> 00:01:41.960
You may have an API product
for general partners

00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:45.050
and a different API product
for closely held partners.

00:01:45.050 --> 00:01:46.717
For example, those
partners that are

00:01:46.717 --> 00:01:48.800
represented with the
business associates agreement

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:49.890
with our company.

00:01:49.890 --> 00:01:53.570
Your product strategy will begin
to shape itself organically

00:01:53.570 --> 00:01:56.870
as your product line grows and
is important that you focus

00:01:56.870 --> 00:01:58.700
on your API consumer needs.

00:01:58.700 --> 00:02:02.000
Consider any digital
transaction you engage in today.

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:06.020
Using your phone to buy coffee,
ordering something online,

00:02:06.020 --> 00:02:08.449
and then tracking
the delivery, or even

00:02:08.449 --> 00:02:10.220
updates in your bank account.

00:02:10.220 --> 00:02:13.100
Each one of these
transactions is an experience

00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:15.140
with an API behind it.

00:02:15.140 --> 00:02:18.470
When you design API products,
you should ask questions like,

00:02:18.470 --> 00:02:21.470
what problems do my
API consumers face?

00:02:21.470 --> 00:02:24.590
How do I make the API
product easy to consume?

00:02:24.590 --> 00:02:27.590
How might you measure the
impact of the solution

00:02:27.590 --> 00:02:29.510
you provided them?

00:02:29.510 --> 00:02:32.610
Taking a broader view,
you might ask yourself,

00:02:32.610 --> 00:02:34.410
how do I get started?

00:02:34.410 --> 00:02:37.610
And then a host of
follow-on questions arise,

00:02:37.610 --> 00:02:40.100
like do I start
designing API products

00:02:40.100 --> 00:02:42.260
for an internal
audience or jump right

00:02:42.260 --> 00:02:44.390
into an external audience?

00:02:44.390 --> 00:02:48.050
Can proxies be reused in
multiple API products?

00:02:48.050 --> 00:02:49.970
Are there policies
that are standard

00:02:49.970 --> 00:02:54.280
across the enterprise that
can be reused in API products?

00:02:54.280 --> 00:02:57.700
The answers to these questions
will form your API product

00:02:57.700 --> 00:03:00.640
strategy, and
furthermore, the strategy

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:02.560
must support your business.

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:06.190
With that said, as you embark
on your API product strategy,

00:03:06.190 --> 00:03:08.320
the best way to help
guide your design

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:10.280
is to provide you
with an example.

00:03:10.280 --> 00:03:13.780
Let's look at the leading ticket
sales and distribution company.

00:03:13.780 --> 00:03:16.120
Their original target
audience for APIs

00:03:16.120 --> 00:03:18.130
were their own
internal developers.

00:03:18.130 --> 00:03:20.770
The company designed the
APIs around event discovery

00:03:20.770 --> 00:03:23.500
and online ticket sales
to enable their developers

00:03:23.500 --> 00:03:26.830
to bring new features to market
faster, and in the process

00:03:26.830 --> 00:03:29.140
quickly found other
benefits when they

00:03:29.140 --> 00:03:31.270
focused on the API consumer.

00:03:31.270 --> 00:03:34.450
APIs were initially focused on
their internal developers who

00:03:34.450 --> 00:03:37.330
were creating applications
for their internal call center

00:03:37.330 --> 00:03:39.760
and online website for
purchasing tickets.

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:43.570
The focus was purely on the
needs of the internal developer

00:03:43.570 --> 00:03:46.060
without much
reusability in mind.

00:03:46.060 --> 00:03:48.040
The top two problems
the company faced

00:03:48.040 --> 00:03:50.110
was how to secure the
connection and the data

00:03:50.110 --> 00:03:52.990
for the application
and how to prevent bots

00:03:52.990 --> 00:03:55.900
from simulating online
purchasers holding tickets

00:03:55.900 --> 00:03:58.480
and thus driving up
the cost of the ticket

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:00.310
due to artificial demand.

00:04:00.310 --> 00:04:02.650
In solving for the
problems, the company

00:04:02.650 --> 00:04:04.780
found that the
security policies could

00:04:04.780 --> 00:04:07.690
be reused by both internal
and partner developers,

00:04:07.690 --> 00:04:10.270
and so they were able to
create API products were using

00:04:10.270 --> 00:04:13.390
the same security policies for
partners, such as Costco, who

00:04:13.390 --> 00:04:16.209
included the ability to search
for events and purchase tickets

00:04:16.209 --> 00:04:17.149
on their website.

00:04:17.149 --> 00:04:19.720
The company found that they
could create API products

00:04:19.720 --> 00:04:22.570
for each trusted partner while
reusing the same security

00:04:22.570 --> 00:04:27.100
policies and demand for their
API products amongst partners

00:04:27.100 --> 00:04:27.820
grew.

00:04:27.820 --> 00:04:29.920
Due to the high
demand, the company

00:04:29.920 --> 00:04:32.080
found that they could engage
additional third party

00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:34.390
supply to help meet the demand.

00:04:34.390 --> 00:04:37.270
Demand continued to grow
with IoT devices coming

00:04:37.270 --> 00:04:38.590
into the picture.

00:04:38.590 --> 00:04:40.360
Consumers wanted to
search for events

00:04:40.360 --> 00:04:42.790
in order tickets
via voice activated

00:04:42.790 --> 00:04:45.040
devices, such as the
Google Home Assistant,

00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:47.470
and thus, an API
product ecosystem

00:04:47.470 --> 00:04:50.680
was born with demand and supply
running through the API product

00:04:50.680 --> 00:04:53.950
ecosystem powered by the
Google Apigee platform.

00:04:53.950 --> 00:04:56.890
The company grew from a
limited supply delivered

00:04:56.890 --> 00:05:00.850
through a closed private
channel to a vibrant marketplace

00:05:00.850 --> 00:05:02.830
with an ecosystem
of API products

00:05:02.830 --> 00:05:05.770
with third party suppliers to
be consumed by new marketing

00:05:05.770 --> 00:05:08.260
channels creating services
that the company didn't

00:05:08.260 --> 00:05:09.340
need to build.

00:05:09.340 --> 00:05:12.430
The added benefit was that
the new services cost little

00:05:12.430 --> 00:05:14.550
to the company to provide.

00:05:14.550 --> 00:05:17.400
There is no one approach to
designing your API product

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:18.210
strategy.

00:05:18.210 --> 00:05:20.040
Rather, there is an
approach that you

00:05:20.040 --> 00:05:23.160
can take to define an
initial API product design

00:05:23.160 --> 00:05:25.580
and refine it over time.

00:05:25.580 --> 00:05:29.837
Your API product strategy
will mature organically.

00:05:29.837 --> 00:05:31.670
It's important that you
don't boil the ocean

00:05:31.670 --> 00:05:32.840
to begin your journey.

00:05:32.840 --> 00:05:34.760
Use customer
journeys and personas

00:05:34.760 --> 00:05:38.000
to design API products that
have a notable business impact

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:40.170
and iterate from there.

00:05:40.170 --> 00:05:42.790
Along your journey, you'll
find repeatable patterns,

00:05:42.790 --> 00:05:44.280
such as the security
rules mandated

00:05:44.280 --> 00:05:45.930
by your corporate
security office

00:05:45.930 --> 00:05:48.750
that you will want to build
out as standard policies

00:05:48.750 --> 00:05:49.740
for re-use.

00:05:49.740 --> 00:05:52.260
It's always a good idea
to design your product

00:05:52.260 --> 00:05:56.190
for external consumers, even
if your initial audience is

00:05:56.190 --> 00:05:57.610
internal.

00:05:57.610 --> 00:06:01.540
Finally, review actual
usage of your API products

00:06:01.540 --> 00:06:04.240
and refine them based on your
metrics and user feedback

00:06:04.240 --> 00:06:06.370
to continuously
improve your product.

00:06:06.370 --> 00:06:09.490
Like your API products,
your product strategy

00:06:09.490 --> 00:06:11.500
will evolve over time.

00:06:11.500 --> 00:06:13.900
Hopefully, you found a few
strategies in this video

00:06:13.900 --> 00:06:16.992
to define your API
product strategy.

00:06:16.992 --> 00:06:17.950
Thank you for watching.

00:06:17.950 --> 00:06:21.000
[MUSIC PLAYING]

